    @section call_reference Call References

    @par Synopsis
    References to functions or object methods are called call references. A call reference can be used like a function pointer; a call reference is a %Qore data type that can be returned by functions or methods or assigned to variables.\n\n
    Note that the empty parentheses after the call are required to identify the expression as a call reference.

    @par Syntax
    <tt>\\</tt><em>function_name</em><tt>()</tt>\n
    <tt>\\</tt><em>class</em><tt>::</tt><em>static_method</em><tt>()</tt>\n
    <tt>\\</tt><em>object.method</em><tt>()</tt>

    @par Description
    <tt>\\</tt> \a function_name <tt>()</tt>\n
    This makes a call reference to a function. The function that the reference names is resolved at parse time; if the function does not exist a parse exception will be thrown.  The reference is to the function, not to one of its variants: if the function is @ref overloading "overloaded", the variant is selected each time the reference is called, with the arguments of that call, from all of the function's variants, wherever they are declared (also after the reference or in another source file of the %Program); if no variant matches the arguments, a \c RUNTIME-OVERLOAD-ERROR exception is thrown.\n\n
    <tt>\\</tt> \a class <tt>::</tt> \a static_method <tt>()</tt>\n
    This makes a call reference to a static method. The static method that the reference names is resolved at parse time; if the static method does not exist a parse exception will be thrown.  As with a reference to a function, the variant of an overloaded static method is selected each time the reference is called, with the arguments of that call.\n\n
    <tt>\\</tt> \a object <tt>.</tt> \a method <tt>()</tt>\n
    - \a object: can be any valid %Qore expression; a value that is not an object gives a reference to a
      @ref call_reference_pseudo_method "pseudo-method"
    - \a method: must be an unquoted string (see example below) and must represent a valid method name of the object's class
      or a pseudo-method of the value.
    .
    This makes a call reference to an object method call, binding the object and the method in the call reference. If the object expression has a class type, the method must exist and be accessible in that class, or be a pseudo-method of objects or a member of the class (a parse exception is thrown otherwise); the method is resolved each time the reference is created, with the class of the object that the expression gives (a subclass can override the method), so a reference expression evaluated for objects of different classes refers to the method of each object's class; the variant of an overloaded method is selected each time the reference is called, with the arguments of that call.  If the object's class has no such method or member and objects have no such pseudo-method (a @ref methodGate_methods "methodGate()" method is not used for a reference), an \c OBJECT-METHOD-REFERENCE-ERROR exception will be thrown; if the method is not accessible where the reference is made, or is the \c constructor(), \c destructor() or \c copy() method of the object's class or of one of its parent classes, an \c ILLEGAL-CALL-REFERENCE exception will be thrown.\n\n
    When called, a call reference to an object method will be executed in the context of the object originally referenced. Object method call references do not prolong the lifetime of an object; if the object is deleted (for example, by going out of scope), then if called the call reference will cause an \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED exception to be thrown.\n\n
    @anchor call_reference_pseudo_method
    A reference made with a value that is not an object calls what a call of the method with the value calls: a
    @ref closure "closure" or call reference in a hash under the name of the method, or the pseudo-method of the
    value's type (<tt>\\l.size()</tt> for a list \c l refers to @ref <list>::size() "<list>::size()").  The
    reference holds the value it was made with: a call does not see a later change of the variable.  If the type of
    the expression cannot have the method, a parse exception is thrown; if the value has none at run time, a
    \c PSEUDO-METHOD-DOES-NOT-EXIST exception, as for a call.  A reference to a method that the class of an object
    does not have, such as the pseudo-method <tt>\\o.className()</tt> or a member holding code, looks the method up
    by name when it is called, as a call does, in the class context of the code that made the reference.  A call
    through the reference takes the arguments of a call of the method, also by name.\n\n
    @anchor call_reference_deleted_object
    The \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED exception of a call through a reference to a deleted object names the class and the
    method that the call runs (<tt>cannot call U::m() on an object that has already been deleted</tt>): the class
    that defines the method of the reference, or, for a reference whose method is looked up by name, the class that
    defines the method found; also when the arguments of the call delete the object, which the call raises after the
    arguments as a direct call does.\n\n
    @anchor call_reference_program
    A call reference to an object method runs as the direct call written where the reference was made: a method of a
    user class runs in its class's @ref Qore::Program "Program" as always, and a builtin method (also one that a user
    class inherits) runs in the %Program that made the reference, with that %Program's parse options and sandbox,
    wherever the reference is called; a reference that the restricted code of one %Program makes and passes to
    another therefore gets no more access than its direct call has, also when the object was created by the code of a
    module, whose own calls run with the module's options (a reference is the code of the %Program that makes it).  A reference to a builtin method that a %Program
    made for an object of another %Program keeps that %Program's data; if the %Program that made it has been deleted
    when the reference is called, a \c PROGRAM-ERROR exception is thrown, as for a @ref closure "closure" created in
    it.

    @par Example
    @code{.py} code c = \printf(); @endcode
    @code{.py} code c = \MyClass::method(); @endcode
    @code{.py} code c = \obj.method(); @endcode
    @code{.py}
sub f(int x) { printf("int: %d\n", x); }
sub f(float x) { printf("float: %f\n", x); }

code c = \f();
# the variant is selected with the arguments of each call
c(1);   # calls f(int)
c(2.5); # calls f(float)
    @endcode

    @note
    - The backslash at the beginning and the empty parentheses at the end; these are required when specifying a call reference.
    - call reference is a code data type; see @ref callref_type for more information

