    @section closure Closures

    @par Synopsis
    A closure is an anonymous function used as a value. Closures can be returned from functions or methods, assigned to variables, or passed as arguments to other functions.

    @par Syntax
    [<b>@ref deprecated "deprecated"</b>] [<b>@ref public_inheritance "public"</b>] [<b>@ref synchronized "synchronized"</b>] <em>@ref data_type_declarations "[return_type]"</em> <tt>\b sub ([</tt><em>@ref data_type_declarations "[type]" variable1</em><tt>, ...]) {</tt> <em>[code]</em> <tt>}</tt>\n\n
    or the alternate (deprecated) syntax with the \c returns keyword after the parameters:\n\n
    [<b>@ref deprecated "deprecated"</b>] [<b>@ref public_inheritance "public"</b>] [<b>@ref synchronized "synchronized"</b>] <tt>\b sub ([</tt> <em>@ref data_type_declarations "[type]" variable1</em><tt>, ...]) \b returns</tt> <em>@ref data_type_declarations "return_type"</em> <tt>{</tt> <em>[code]</em> <tt>}</tt>

    @par Description
    Closures encapsulate the state and value of local variables of the outer code block referenced from within the closure when the closure is created. Whenever local variables are bound within a closure, these variables are subject to concurrent thread access protection (locking) just as with global variables, in order to allow closures to be used in any context without restriction and to preseve thread-safety regarding bound local variables.\n\n
    Note that returning a closure from within an object method encapsulates the state of the object as well (it's legal to refer to \c self and to object members from within closures created from objects), but the closure does not prolong the scope of the object: once the object has been destroyed (for example a temporary object as in <tt>new MyClass().getClosure()</tt>, which is destroyed at the end of the statement that created it, so a closure that the statement calls still sees it, but one that is called by a later statement does not), \c self evaluates to no value in the closure and member accesses and method calls raise \c OBJECT-ALREADY-DELETED exceptions; see @ref object_scope "object scope" and @ref temporary_lifetime "the lifetime of temporary values".\n\n
    @anchor closure_default_values
    The default values of a closure's parameters are evaluated when the closure is called, also after the code that
    created it has returned: the local variables of the outer code block that they refer to are bound to the closure
    as those of its code are, and in a closure created in an object method they are in the context of the closure's
    object, as its code is (they can refer to \c self and to the object's members and methods, also to change them,
    as in <tt>auto sub (auto x = (self.a = 20)) { return x; }</tt>, and they do not prolong the scope of the object
    either).  \c self is the closure's own there, as in its code: a default value can also take a reference to it or to
    a member through it (ex: <tt>\\self.a</tt>), and a write to \c self itself through such a reference leaves the
    closure's object unchanged.\n\n
    Note that parameter and return types are required when the @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_TYPES or @ref Qore::PO_REQUIRE_PROTOTYPES parse options are set.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# if b is a local variable in the function where the closure is created
# then b will be bound to the closure when the closure is created
code closure = int sub (int a) { return a + b; };
    @endcode

    @note closure is a code data type; see @ref closure_type for more information

    <hr>
    @section implicit_arguments Implicit Argument References

    @par Synopsis
    Implicit arguments are arguments not captured by parameter variables as well as automatic arguments in list-processing operator expressions. A special syntax to reference these arguments is documented here.

    @par Syntax
    <tt>$</tt><em>int</em>   (for a single implicit argument; \a int is the argument number, where \c 1 is the first argument)\n
    <tt>$$</tt>              (for the entire implicit argument list)

    @par Description
    Implicit arguments can be directly referenced using the dollar sign (<tt>$</tt>) and either a number from \c 1 onwards (giving the position in the argument list, where \c 1 is the first element) or a double dollar sign (<tt>$$</tt>) giving the entire implicit argument list.\n\n
    For unassigned arguments to functions or methods, this syntax supplements the automatic argv variable holding all function arguments not assigned to parameter variables.\n\n
    This syntax is particularly useful when writing expressions for the @ref map "map", @ref foldl "foldl", @ref foldr "foldr", and @ref select "select" operators, where implicit argument references are the only way the operator expressions can reference the current list values that are populated as implicit arguments as the operators traverse the list.

    @anchor implicit_argument_offsets
    The argument number is decimal; leading zeros are ignored: <tt>$08</tt> is <tt>$8</tt> and <tt>$010</tt> is
    <tt>$10</tt>, unlike an integer literal with a leading zero, which is octal.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# extract a list of even numbers from a list
list l = select list, !($1 % 2);
    @endcode

    <hr>
    @section implicit_index Implicit Index

    @par Synopsis
    The current list index position when implicitly iterating through lists can be referenced using the implicit index reference characters: <tt>$#</tt>.

    @par Syntax
    <tt>$#</tt>

    @par Description
    The implicit index reference expression (<tt>$#</tt>) gives the index of the element being processed in the code
    that a @ref foreach "foreach statement" runs for each element (its body), and in the code that the
    @ref map "map" (also with a select expression), @ref hmap "hash map" and @ref select "select" operators and the
    predicates of the @ref streaming_first_section "first", @ref streaming_any_section "any",
    @ref streaming_all_section "all", @ref streaming_count_section "count",
    @ref streaming_takewhile_section "takewhile" and @ref streaming_takeuntil_section "takeuntil" operators run for
    each element.  An operator over a single value (a value that is not a list or an iterator) gives it the index
    \c 0, as for a list of one element.

    <tt>$#</tt> belongs to the operator or statement whose per-element code encloses it in the same function, also
    in a statement (such as a while loop) nested in that code.  An operator in a function, method or closure called
    from that code gives its own elements their index, and the caller's <tt>$#</tt> is the same when the call
    returns.

    <tt>$#</tt> anywhere else is a parse error (\c IMPLICIT-INDEX-NOT-ALLOWED) in every execution mode: in the body
    of a while, for or do-while loop or of a context statement that is not itself in such per-element code; in the
    body of a function, method or closure, also one called from or defined in per-element code; in the expression of
    the @ref foldl "foldl" and @ref foldr "foldr" operators, which do not set it, also in per-element code; in the
    iterated expression of an operator or foreach statement that is not itself in per-element code; in a default
    argument value or a member, constant or global variable initializer; and in top-level code outside these
    constructs, also after a foreach statement has ended.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
# create a list of indexes with negative values
list l = map $#, list, ($1 < 0);

# an operator in a function called from a map body gives its own elements their index: gives ((0, 1), (0, 1))
auto sub get_indexes() { return map $#, (7, 8); }
list l2 = map get_indexes(), (1, 2);

# a parse error (IMPLICIT-INDEX-NOT-ALLOWED): a while loop does not set the implicit index
while (stmt.next()) {
    printf("%d\n", $#);
}
    @endcode
*/
